Based on this research — the spring-aspects 6.0.0 POM pulls the same `aspectjweaver:1.9.9.1` as before, its sibling `spring-context`/`spring-beans`/`spring-core` 6.0.0 add no new transitive runtime requirement (no jakarta annotation API, no servlet/db dependency), there's no Docker image associated with this library anywhere in the allow-list, and the existing `build.gradle` already parameterizes the library version so no dependency coordinates need to change for the bump. The native-image build itself completes successfully in the log (analysis, universe, compilation, image creation all succeed) — only the `nativeTest` execution exits 1, and the log is truncated before showing which JUnit test failed or why, so there's no concrete evidence pointing to a missing dependency, image, or environment/system property. That also means I can't satisfy the strict FAILURE→TRACE→CLASSIFY→SOLE PATH→RANGE chain for an unsupportable verdict (I can't even name the failing operation), so this stays repairable by default rather than skip_unsupported.

